\name{Ggplot2PercentileBandLayerProcessor}
\alias{Ggplot2PercentileBandLayerProcessor}
\title{Percentile Band Layer Processor}
\description{
Reads a fan of nested quantile intervals around a median -- the
\code{stat_lineribbon()} / \code{geom_lineribbon()} of ggdist -- as the
\code{percentile_band} trace: at each x, the distribution's quantiles, which a
reader enters on the median and walks up and down band by band, each
announced as the band it bounds ("Middle 80\% is 1.2 to 3.4").

Until this, a lineribbon matched no branch of the adapter, and an unread
layer drops the whole chart to a static image.

\strong{What is read.} ggdist draws one ribbon per interval width and the
median line over them. A ribbon of width \code{w} spans the quantiles
\code{(1 - w) / 2} and \code{(1 + w) / 2}, and the line is the quantile \code{0.5} --
which holds only for a median with quantile intervals (\code{median_qi()},
\code{stat_lineribbon()}'s default). A mean, or a highest-density interval, is
not a quantile, and \code{\link[=lineribbon_quantile_rows]{lineribbon_quantile_rows()}} declines the layer before
it reaches this processor.

\strong{Selectors.} One per band, outermost first, then the median line: the
shape the core reads as "one element per band". ggdist draws its ribbons
widest first, so the draw order of the band polygons is already the order
asked for; a count that disagrees with the widths emits no selectors
rather than mispaired ones.

\strong{A \code{median_hilow} ribbon.} \code{stat_summary(geom = "ribbon", fun.data = median_hilow)} is a band of one width, \code{fun.args$conf.int}, around the
median the stat computed, and is read the same way from
\code{\link[=summary_band_rows]{summary_band_rows()}}. Its selectors are the ribbon's one polygon and,
when a \code{stat_summary()} median line sits on it, that line's polyline;
the line layer itself is skipped, its values being the band's median.

Emitted with \code{type = "percentile_band"}, which the core has read since
maidr 4.14.0.
}
